ent, 2) explain why it fails in this specific context: the decision is personal, emotional, and the parents' evaluation of their own son will override any demographic stereotype, and 3) reiterate the practical advice: don't try to win the argument by saying "young people are bad with money," because it won't work on parents who love him. Instead, frame it as lack of specific experience, or if you have examples, use those. The final challenge is to get the user to see that even if they're right, they still need to communicate it effectively. I'll craft a response that directly tackles the insurance analogy.
